# Pseudos — a name you choose, beside the id you cannot

Every node is identified by its `NodeID`: twenty bytes derived from its ML-DSA
public key. It is unique, unforgeable, and impossible to choose. It is also
`73b7474e2578749edb2df725030e6e12fce5c453`, which nobody wants to read.

A **pseudo** is the name shown next to it. You pick it, you change it whenever
you like, and it appears everywhere the node does: the console, chat, fleet.

Code: [`src/pseudo.py`](../../src/pseudo.py) (the form),
[`src/pseudo_dir.py`](../../src/pseudo_dir.py) (the signed claim and the book),
the pseudo section of [`src/node.py`](../../src/node.py) (gossip, directory,
search).

## What a pseudo is not

It is **not an identity**, and treating it as one is the mistake this whole
design is built to prevent.

- Pseudos are **not unique**. Two nodes may both be called `alice`, and neither
  is more entitled to it than the other. A search returns them all.
- A pseudo decides **nothing**. Routing, authentication, trust and permissions
  all key on the node id. Renaming a node changes what people read and nothing
  else.
- So a pseudo is **always shown with its id**. Every list, every dialog, every
  search result carries both. If you are about to trust a node, check the id.

What a pseudo *does* guarantee is that nobody can put a name on **your** node.
A name travels as a claim signed by the identity it names, and the id in that
claim is derived from the key that signed it — so a claim can only ever name its
own author. Somebody may call themselves `alice`; nobody can make your node say
it is `alice`.

## Choosing one

**In the console:** Settings → Identity → *What this node is called*. Type it,
press Save. It takes effect immediately — the node signs a new claim and tells
the mesh — and it is written to the configuration file so a restart keeps it.

**At startup:** `--pseudo "Alice Ada"`, or in `nmesh.conf`:

```
pseudo = Alice Ada
```

**In chat:** the *Display name* field in the settings dialog is the same value.
There is no separate chat name any more.

**Removing it** leaves the node showing only its id — here. Other nodes keep the
last name they were told until they forget it: a claim is a statement that this
node *is* called something, and there is no signed way to say "I am called
nothing" (an empty name is not a name). If you want the old one gone from other
people's screens, rename rather than clear — a rename does propagate, because it
is itself a claim.

### What is allowed

At most **50 characters** (characters, not bytes — accents and CJK cost one
each). Letters, digits, punctuation, spaces between words, emoji.

Refused, deliberately:

- anything invisible or directional — zero-width spaces and joiners,
  left-to-right and right-to-left marks, control characters. These exist to make
  one name render as another;
- spaces that are not the plain space `U+0020` (no-break, ideographic…), for the
  same reason;
- leading, trailing or doubled spaces, and an empty name.

A name with a run of spaces in the middle is tidied to single spaces rather than
refused: that is a typing slip, not an attack. Everything else is reported back
to you, unchanged, with what is wrong.

**These rules are checked on both sides.** A node that receives a name in any
other form does not clean it up — it drops it and counts the sender as
misbehaving, and cuts the link if it keeps happening. See
[`Docs/Architecture/security.md`](../Architecture/security.md).

## Finding somebody

Settings → Identity → *Find a node by name*. Type as much as you know:

- `alice ada` — an exact match ranks first;
- `ali` — anything starting with it;
- `ada` — a word inside the name;
- `jose` finds `José`, and case never matters.

Results are ranked best-first and each carries its node id and a link to the
node's details. The **Connected nodes** and **Known nodes** tables filter on names
too, so the search box you already use works on them.

This all answers instantly, because it reads names this node has already
learned. Names spread by gossip: when two nodes connect they exchange the names
they know, and a rename ripples outward from there. So a mesh you are part of
fills in on its own.

**Ask the network** does one more thing: a directory lookup for the name exactly
as typed, which finds somebody whose node you have never met. It costs a round
of queries, so it is a button rather than something typing triggers.

## For apps

An app talks to the node through the connector. It can read names and search
them; it **cannot set one**:

```python
await client.my_pseudo()                    # what is this node called?
await client.lookup_pseudo("ali")           # ranked, partial, from the local book
await client.lookup_pseudo("alice", wide=True)   # …and ask the network too
await client.pseudos_of([id_hex, ...])      # resolve ids to names, in one call
await client.refresh_names(ids)             # the same, into a cache
client.name_of(id_hex)                      # read that cache, synchronously
```

There is deliberately no frame that renames the node. The pseudo is shown in the
node's console and used by every app on it, so choosing it belongs to whoever
runs the node — not to an app that happens to hold a connector token. Renaming
goes through the console (`POST /api/pseudo`), which is authenticated.

`refresh_names` and `name_of` are one cache shared by every app on a client:
the alternative is each app keeping its own idea of what a node is called, which
is the thing pseudos exist to stop. Chat and Fleet both use it.

## What it costs

A claim is about 5.3 kB — an ML-DSA-65 public key and signature, which is what
makes it verifiable by a node that has never met you. Each node keeps a bounded
book of them (`_MAX_NODES` entries, `_MAX_BOOK_BYTES` bytes, whichever binds
first), evicting the least recently touched. A freshly connected peer is sent at
most `_PSEUDO_SYNC_MAX` claims, ours first; the rest arrives by gossip.

Gossip stops on its own: a node passes a claim on **only when it changed what it
knew**, so a name that has finished spreading stops moving.

